      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Hidetoshi Katori",
        "Jun Ye"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Optical lattice clocks turned precision spectroscopy into a platform for exceptionally accurate timekeeping and tests of fundamental physics, with broad influence across atomic physics and metrology.",
        "attribution": "Katori and Ye made distinct, complementary contributions to the optical lattice clock and its experimental realization.",
        "maturity": "The method has been repeatedly demonstrated and refined over decades; its scientific impact and practical prospects are clear.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The balance between foundational invention and the wider community's role in advancing clock performance deserves careful review."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 2,
      "ballot_id": "B016",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Juan Ignacio Cirac",
        "Peter Zoller",
        "Rainer Blatt"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The trapped-ion program established a powerful route to quantum logic and computation, connecting influential theoretical proposals to precise control of individual atoms.",
        "attribution": "Cirac and Zoller provided central theoretical foundations, while Blatt led landmark experimental demonstrations; the three contributions are complementary.",
        "maturity": "The field has developed through sustained experimental progress, with trapped ions among the most mature quantum-computing platforms.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The achievement spans theory, demonstration and later engineering advances, so the prize's precise scope would need definition."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 3,
      "ballot_id": "B054",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Eugene S. Polzik",
        "H. Jeff Kimble",
        "Mikhail D. Lukin"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Light-matter interfaces and atomic quantum memories made it possible to store and retrieve quantum states of light, an important step toward quantum networks and scalable quantum communication.",
        "attribution": "Polzik, Kimble and Lukin represent influential, complementary approaches to interfaces and memories; the broader field also includes substantial contributions from other groups.",
        "maturity": "The core effects and memory protocols are well established, while long-distance, high-efficiency network integration remains an active challenge.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The breadth of the field makes it difficult to draw a narrow boundary around the prizeworthy discovery and its principal contributors."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 4,
      "ballot_id": "B005",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Antoine Browaeys",
        "Immanuel Bloch",
        "Mikhail D. Lukin"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Programmable Rydberg arrays and optical-lattice experiments opened new ways to simulate and control many-body quantum systems, with results that connect quantum optics to condensed matter physics.",
        "attribution": "Browaeys, Bloch and Lukin have made leading contributions across complementary experimental approaches to quantum simulation.",
        "maturity": "The platforms have produced reproducible, expanding bodies of work, though their reach as general-purpose simulators is still developing.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The rapid pace of progress makes it early to judge which results will prove most transformative."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 5,
      "ballot_id": "B050",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Carlton M. Caves",
        "H. Jeff Kimble",
        "Roman Schnabel"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Squeezed light provides a concrete way to reduce measurement noise below the standard quantum limit and has become important in precision interferometry, including gravitational-wave detection.",
        "attribution": "Caves supplied foundational theory, Kimble helped establish key experimental methods, and Schnabel advanced the technology toward high-performance interferometers.",
        "maturity": "The technique has been demonstrated in increasingly capable instruments and has delivered measurable gains in gravitational-wave observatories.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The work spans several generations of theory and experiment, and the selection of a small set of laureates would require a careful account of later contributions."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 6,
      "ballot_id": "B001",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Eli Yablonovitch",
        "Sajeev John",
        "John D. Joannopoulos"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Photonic crystals and band gaps gave physicists a powerful way to control light propagation, reshaping photonics and enabling a wide range of later devices and experiments.",
        "attribution": "Yablonovitch and John independently articulated central ideas, and Joannopoulos helped develop the theoretical and computational foundations that made the field accessible.",
        "maturity": "The concepts are well established and have had lasting influence, although applications vary in their reach and maturity.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The discovery has several independent origins and the credited list includes important contributions to distinct strands, making the award's scope consequential."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 7,
      "ballot_id": "B047",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Christian J. Bordé",
        "David E. Pritchard",
        "Mark A. Kasevich"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Matter-wave atom interferometry created a sensitive measurement method for gravity, acceleration and rotations, extending precision measurement with atomic coherence.",
        "attribution": "Bordé, Pritchard and Kasevich made distinct foundational contributions to atom interferometry and its precision-measurement applications.",
        "maturity": "The method has been demonstrated across laboratory and field settings, with substantial continuing work on robustness and practical deployment.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The field's impact is distributed across multiple interferometer types and later experimental groups."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 8,
      "ballot_id": "B004",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Fabiola Gianotti",
        "Peter Jenni",
        "Tejinder Virdee"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The experimental discovery of the Higgs boson was a landmark confirmation of a central part of particle physics and depended on detectors capable of finding a rare signal amid enormous backgrounds.",
        "attribution": "Gianotti, Jenni and Virdee represent leadership and detector-building contributions to ATLAS and CMS, but the discovery was the work of two very large collaborations.",
        "maturity": "The signal has been confirmed and characterized through extensive follow-up measurements over many years.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "Attributing a collaboration-scale discovery to a handful of individuals is inherently difficult and risks obscuring the work of thousands of contributors."
        ]
      }
    }
  ]
